===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|en|ang|lēoht}}, {{m|ang|līht}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*lį̄ht}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*linhtaz}} or {{m|gem-pro|*līhtaz}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁lengʷʰ-||light}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|licht}}, {{cog|de|leicht}}, {{cog|sv|lätt}}, {{cog|no|lett}}, {{cog|sq|lehtë}}, {{cog|la|levis}}, {{cog|ru|лёгкий}}, {{cog|lt|lengvas}}, {{cog|sa|लघु}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# Having [[little]] or relatively little actual [[weight]]; not [[cumbrous]] or [[unwieldy]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''light''' load ; a '''lighter''' backpack after having removed the books ; '''light''' weapons}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|V|463|21 August 1712}}
#*: These weights did not exert their natural gravity {{...}} insomuch that I could not guess which was '''light''' or heavy whilst I held them in my hand.
# Having little weight as compared with [[bulk]]; of little [[density]] or [[specific gravity]].
#: {{ux|en|feathers and cork are '''light''' ; oil is '''lighter''' than water}}
# Of [[short]] or [[insufficient]] weight; weighing less than the [[legal]], [[standard]]{{,}} or [[proper]] amount; [[clipped]] or [[diminished]].
#: {{ux|en|to issue '''light''' coin}}
# Lacking that which burdens or makes heavy.
## Free from [[burden]] or [[impediment]]; [[unencumbered]].
## Lightly built; typically designed for speed or small loads.
##: {{ux|en|a '''light''' aircraft ; a '''light''' tank}}
## {{lb|en|military}} Not heavily [[armed]]; armed with light [[weapon]]s.
##: {{ux|en|'''light''' infantry; a troop of '''light''' horse}}
## {{lb|en|nautical|of a [[ship]]}} [[ride|Riding]] high because of no [[cargo]]; {{n-g|by extension,}} pertaining to a ship which is light.
##: {{ux|en|if a ship is '''light''' or partially loaded ; the '''light''' draft of a vessel, or its '''light''' displacement}}
## {{lb|en|rail transport|of a [[locomotive]] or [[consist]] of locomotives}} Without any piece of equipment attached or attached only to a [[caboose]].
##: {{ux|en|the '''light''' locomotives ; a locomotive may be moved '''light'''}}
## With low [[viscosity]].
# {{lb|en|cookery}} Not heavy or [[soggy]]; [[spongy]]; well [[raise]]d.
#: {{ux|en|a '''light''' bread ; sponge cake is a '''light''' cake}}
# [[low|Low]] in fat, calories, alcohol, salt, etc.
#: ''This '''light''' beer still gets you drunk if you have enough of it.''
# [[slight|Slight]], not forceful or intense; small in amount or intensity.
#: ''a '''light''' drizzle''; ''a '''light''' rain was falling''; ''a '''light''' snow set in''
# {{senseid|en|having little force or momentum}}Gentle; having little force or momentum.
#: ''This artist clearly had a '''light''', flowing touch.''
# Easy to endure or perform.
#: '''''light''' duties around the house''
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Palamon and Arcite}}
#*: '''Light''' sufferings give us leisure to complain.
# Unimportant, [[trivial]], having little value or significance.
#: ''I made some '''light''' comment, and we moved on.''
#* '''1902''', John Buchan, ''The Outgoing of the Tide'':
#*: He had drunk more than was fit for him, and he was singing some '''light''' song, when he saw approaching, as he said, the pale horse mentioned in the Revelation, with Death seated as the rider.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[unchaste|Unchaste]], [[wanton]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|I|i|passage=Long after lay he musing at her mood, / Much grieu'd to thinke that gentle Dame so '''light''', / For whose defence he was to shed his blood.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|V|ii|passage=So do not you; for you are a '''light''' girl.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|V|i|passage=A '''light''' wife doth make a heavy husband.}}
# Not encumbered; unembarrassed; clear of impediments; hence, active; nimble; swift.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Marriage and Single Life|passage=Unmarried men are best friends, best masters {{...}} but not always best subjects, for they are '''light''' to run away.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} Easily influenced by trifling considerations; unsteady; unsettled; volatile.
#: ''a '''light''', vain person; a '''light''' mind''
#* '''1633''', {{w|John Tillotson}}, ''The Wisdom of being Religious''
#*: There is no greater argument of a '''light''' and inconsiderate person than profanely to scoff at religion.
# Indulging in, or inclined to, levity; lacking dignity or solemnity; frivolous; airy.
#: ''Ogden Nash was a writer of '''light''' verse.''
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|II|ii|passage=Seneca can not be too heavy, nor Plautus too '''light'''.}}
#* '''1851''', {{w|Nathaniel Hawthorne}}, ''Old News''
#*: specimens of New England humour laboriously '''light''' and lamentably mirthful
# Not quite sound or normal; somewhat impaired or deranged; dizzy; giddy.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|IV|i|passage=Are his wits safe? Is he not '''light''' of brain?}}
# Easily interrupted by stimulation.
#: '''''light''' sleep; '''light''' anesthesia''

